\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Eloise\'92s cavalcade slid out of the Ghetto and joined the traffic on an arterial road like a shiver of sharks among bait fish. They neither sped nor slowed as commuter fear and sympathetically manipulated traffic lights eased their progress out of the Pale. Once over the council border, they took a slip-road towards a hover-pad stalk looming like a grey stand of celery, its leaves occluded in the Layer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The ground cars approached tangentially, taking a curving ramp off the public road into a tunnel access. No boom gates or doors, no guardhouse, no apparent surveillance equipment. The only distinguishing feature of the square tunnel mouth was an engraved inscription in its concrete lintel:
\fs20  SHAKTI ENTERPRISES
\fs22 . Those words were all the security needed, connoting all kinds of threats and forewarnings. \'93Private Property. Trespassers will become the subject of unfortunate anecdotes.\'94 There was a rumour Eloise had people dropped in a huge, deep bath of concentrated Layer chemicals, which corroded away all trace of them. Eloise had started the rumour herself, finding that a good reputation for creative homicide saved considerable time on paperwork and reduced the need for actual creative homicide. But that wasn\'92t true, was it? Sarah wondered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The tunnel curved in a gently tightening spiral until it opened abruptly into a large dock on the left. Ahead stretched a high-ceiling garage for an uncountable number of ground vehicles. It was empty. Eloise\'92s car eased to a halt within steps of a stainless steel elevator, polished to a gentle, immutable gleam. Its doors opened as Eloise alighted, revealing a hold large enough for two football teams, their reserves, the referees, coaches, support staff and several dozen of their greatest fans.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise ushered the women inside. As Alexander attempted to follow, she held out her hand to block him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Mother!\'94 Alexander objected like a boy having his toys taken away. His eyes were huge and wet, his eyebrows tangled over his nose.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise fussed with Alexander\'92s collar. \'93Be patient, my darling. We have wedding plans to make.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Really?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of course. Now, I think it\'92s time you thought about syndicating Candid Criminal internationally.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, yes, we\'92ve been thinking about Russia\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93See, you\'92re already ahead of me.\'94 Eloise kissed his cheek.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander sought out Sarah\'92s eyes. Sarah froze, knowing to look away would sabotage Eloise\'92s gambit. She gulped and allowed her head to nod in a flutter.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander exhaled in relief as the elevator doors closed centimetres from his nose.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The elevator delivered them to a corridor lined with floor-to-ceiling glass, offering views of the city that made Sarah feel like she was floating above the world. At the far end waited a sleek hover-copt, its rotors already spinning lazily in preparation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I need to collect Chainsaw,\'94 Hayley announced, stopping short of the aircraft.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise\'92s immaculate composure flickered for perhaps a nanosecond. \'93You want a chainsaw? I assure you I have grislier weapons.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not \'91a chainsaw\'92. Chainsaw, my cat. I\'92m not leaving without him.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The pause that followed could have been measured with geological instruments. Eloise consulted her watch\'97a piece that probably cost more than most people earned in a year\'97and performed some internal calculation involving schedules, security windows, and the peculiar madness of civilians who prioritised pets over survival.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Where,\'94 she said finally, \'93does one collect this Chainsaw?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My flat. Ten minutes, I promise.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ten minutes,\'94 Eloise repeated, as if testing the words for hidden toxins. \'93Very well.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Thirty-seven minutes later, Hayley emerged from her building wrestling with a cat box that was producing sounds reminiscent of bagpipes and a cockatoo being murdered in a wood chipper. The box lurched and bucked in her grip like it contained a small, furious demon rather than a domestic pet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i You
\f1\i0  should try getting a devon rex into a carrier,\'94 Hayley gasped as she approached the hover-copt, sweat beading on her forehead despite the cool evening air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise\'92s expression suggested she had already considered this exact scenario and filed it under \'93experiences I shall never require.\'94 Without a word, she held out her hands. Hayley, defeated by forty minutes of combat with her pet, surrendered the carrier.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise set it down with the reverence of a bomb disposal expert. Through the mesh door, Sarah glimpsed something that looked like a bat had been crossbred with a particularly neurotic space alien\'97all ears and eyes and indignant superiority.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The moment Eloise opened the carrier, Chainsaw launched himself toward what he clearly perceived as freedom. Mid-flight, Eloise intercepted him with movements so fluid they seemed choreographed. She cradled him against her immaculate jacket, one hand supporting his hindquarters, the other his chest, holding him at a precise forty-five-degree angle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then she began to sway, a gentle, hypnotic rhythm, like kelp in a slow current.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Shh,\'94 she murmured, her voice dropping to barely a whisper. Her fingers found the sensitive spots behind Chainsaw\'92s oversized ears and began a circular massage that seemed to drain the fight out of him like water from a sink.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Devon rexes,\'94 she observed conversationally, never breaking her rhythm, \'93are particularly neurotic. Due to their selective breeding. High intelligence, low tolerance for chaos. The key is becoming the most boring, predictable element in their environment.\'94 She demonstrated by gradually slowing her sway until she was nearly motionless. \'93Make yourself a lighthouse\'97steady, dependable, insufferably dull.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley huffed: as if Eloise would ever describe herself as any of those things.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chainsaw\'92s protests subsided to confused chirps, then to a tentative purr that vibrated through Eloise\'92s jacket like a small, contented engine.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Now,\'94 Eloise said, stepping into the hover-copt with Chainsaw draped across her shoulder like a furry, purring scarf, \'93we travel.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah and Kathy exchanged glances.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The hover-copt lifted into the sunset, luxury leather warming beneath them as the city fell away. Half an hour later it descended through dusk towards a ground pad with blocky white words painted across its landing circle, easily read from hundreds of metres away: SUBLIME POINT.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Eloise\'92s residential compound. Probably one of the safest locations in the country.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As they approached, Sarah saw amorphous glassy flashes twinkling from flat, rocky space surrounded by pentagonal bush garden. Then, as if the building had been waiting for an audience worth impressing, a palace resolved out of the rock\'97a multi-storey triumph of modern materials that looked like Cinderella\'92s Castle had converted to Islam during its golden age of astronomy and poetry. It was constructed of interweaving triangles of sweeping, almost four-dimensional curves of bush-smoked glass, held together by impossibly wrought slender tendrils of advanced metal alloys.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Five tall structures grew from its heart in a perfect pentagon. At first Sarah thought they were trees, then they flickered like failing holograms and became minarets\'97lattices of metal spiralling to tapering points.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It would not be out of place on the cover of a futuristic sci-fi fantasy novel, the kind where the cover artist had more imagination than the author. There would be an advanced civilisation, Sarah mused, who conquered the universe by shaming other worlds into submission for their inadequate feats of architecture.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The hover-copt landed. A waiting ground car rolled to within metres.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Two uniformed guard-type women blocked Sarah and the others as they started following Eloise towards the palace. \'93Please accompany us,\'94 one said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise turned. \'93You\'92ll be well looked after. They\'92ll get you settled in for the evening.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy and Hayley squared off against Eloise\'92s girl goons. \'93Are we free to leave if we wish?\'94 Hayley asked, sounding like her whole existence was made for conducting investigations.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise threw her hands out in an encompassing, inclusive gesture. \'93Of course. But I think you\'92ll all agree, given today\'92s events, you\'92re safest here. Stay the night. You\'92ll be accommodated in luxury, I assure you. We\'92ll talk in the morning and assess what you want to do next.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s all right,\'94 Sarah placated. Perhaps it was no more than Airborne confirmation bias, but if she was still wary of Eloise, she trusted this place, with all its statements of wealth and power.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy and Hayley exchanged looks of shared suspicion. Kathy gestured to Sarah: I go where she goes. Hayley stiffened, watched the pair turn for the second car, then relented and followed, a few steps behind, Chainsaw\'92s carrier swinging gently at her side.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The road wound through native bushland and what looked like a stately university campus\'97grey one-storey buildings nestled under towering eucalypts, camouflaged by the materials and shapes of their roofing. Far more buildings than could be seen from the air. Sarah wondered about the widow-maker branches that would inevitably drop from the trees; the buildings must be sturdy. And bushfire-proof.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The car circled a roundabout and stopped outside one of three townhouses reaching towards the road like splayed fingers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Come on,\'94 the driver said as she got out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They walked a curved path towards an inviting portico, subtly lit with warm lighting that intensified as they approached. At the front door, the driver waved her hand over a panel in the exterior brick. The door clicked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ll be staying here,\'94 the driver said to Sarah. \'93You have the next townhouse,\'94 she told Kathy and Hayley.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you locking us in?\'94 Hayley asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No. But be careful you don\'92t inadvertently lock yourselves outside.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah found the way the driver said 
\f2\i inadvertently
\f1\i0  disconcerting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92ll sort out proper access in the morning. For now, please enjoy the hospitality of Eloise Shakti. I\'92m sure you\'92ll find every comfort and luxury you could possibly want for the evening. Inside.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy reached for Sarah\'92s elbow. \'93It\'92s all right. We\'92ll see you in the morning.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nodded and walked over the threshold.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Less than fifteen minutes later Sarah heard a knock at the door.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 There stood Kathy and Hayley, Chainsaw\'92s carrier at Hayley\'92s feet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We seem to have inadvertently locked ourselves outside,\'94 Hayley said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well first it was me,\'94 Kathy explained. \'93Then, when I knocked on the door, Hayley got herself locked out as well.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Inadvertently,\'94 Hayley added, picking up the carrier.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Through the mesh, Chainsaw regarded the proceedings with the weary resignation of an advanced being who, long ago, had accepted humans were fundamentally untrainable.
\f0\fs24 <!$Scr_Ps::1>}